    "language": [],
    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "As Artificial Intelligence (AI) permeates our daily lives and transforms the workforce around the globe, AI education is increasingly recognized as essential at the K-12 level. In fact, according to UNESCO, 16 countries are already developing K-12 AI curriculum standards. However, high school students currently face challenges in learning how AI works, both in and out of school, because there are very limited learning resources that are suitable for their level. On one hand, no-code tools, such as those built on the Scratch environment, treat AI as a black box and do not help students to learn their underlying mechanisms, and on the other hand, code-based solutions, such as Jupyter notebooks, are too advanced for high school students, most of which have limited Computer Science knowledge, to fully digest.\r\n\r\nAI Playground addresses this problem through an interactive visual programming environment and learning platform for students to learn, build, and share their works of AI. In the self-paced curriculum, students learn AI by creating neural networks to program a teachable agent, Synthia, who they can interact with and whose capabilities grow as they progress in their learning journey. Every exercise is accompanied by automatic checks to ensure real-time feedback. Students can apply their skills to solve interesting real-world machine-learning problems by creating their own projects using the available datasets. They could also view projects that their peers have created and made public. Preliminary prototype tests with nearly 40 G8-12 students showed promising results that students overall liked AI Playground and could learn effectively with it.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Shan, J. (2024). AI Playground - report and video. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/hw294hw1958. https://doi.org/10.25740/hw294hw1958.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
